WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Crazed Colonel Vermin Katafi poses new terrorist threat to the world.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:27.640
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game in North America.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.620
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.940
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.280
Guys, today we are talking about Metal Gear.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.560
Metal Gear? Sean, you're a resident Metal Gear expert.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.480
Do you want to say anything to start the show?

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.200
I feel like I shouldn't be starting this episode.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.680
I really, I wouldn't say that I'm the expert on Metal Gear.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.540
Among us. I probably played it more than the rest of the people in this room.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:01:01.160
I have avoided Metal Gear in my life so much, but I'm so adamant about not having anything spoiled for me that I have never, like, I've never looked at anything for Metal Gear.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.380
You don't even want to play it and have it spoiled that way.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:07.920
It's crazy. Like, I don't know anything other than that, like, maybe he has a clone or a twin or a younger brother.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:08.940
I don't, that's all I know.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.040
Well, you see, Solid Snake is actually a clone of Big Boss and a big boss.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.500
That would be crazy.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.160
Even more so, what's funny is that I do know all of these, like, Metal Gear memes, though.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.940
Like, that long ladder in Metal Gear 3.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.000
That's not a meme. That is a meme because, who is it?

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.680
Dunkey just did a whole thing on that, like, last year or two years ago.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.620
Sure. And it, like, became a thing again about, like, why was that ladder so long and, like.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.120
It's called storytelling, Mike.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:42.060
Okay. And ambitious design philosophies.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:48.400
Got it. Well, that ladder would be just, like, the longest ladder in Death Stranding, right?

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.580
Like, Death Stranding is a game where you can have the ladder be as long as you want it.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:59.160
As long as you want. Really, this episode more than Metal Gear is actually about a little guy named Hideo Kojima.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:00.640
True. Hideo.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:01.900
Hideo, I go, actually.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.420
I always did Hideo when I was young.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.720
Hideo. No, it is.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.720
But I just think that's funny if, like, I keep it to my world.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.740
I don't want to do any immersion breaking for myself.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.380
Same with your Mario bullshit.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.000
Yeah. Mario? You say Mario?

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:13.920
I used to be a Mario.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.360
You used to say Mario. I used to be a Mario guy.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.740
And then, like, I don't know. Enough people started saying Mario.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:18.980
And I was like, it's fine.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.900
Right? It's not Mario Lopez.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.580
So, it is Mario Lopez.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.260
So, like, I'm cool with it.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.360
It's fine. That was the final straw.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:27.960
That's, like, the comparison.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.080
That's the litmus test.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.100
Anyway. Yeah.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.140
Anyway, Kojima. You got to have some thoughts on him, too, right?

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.960
I very much appreciate Hideo Kojima.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:43.180
I think he's got a lot of great ideas, and he really doesn't know how to tell them to people.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.480
But I think, you know, it's like one of those misunderstood artists things that's just sort of cute.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.500
Okay. Yeah. I never take any of his stories all that seriously, but he really knows how to...

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.860
It is entrancing stuff.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:03.800
This is arguably the third, or maybe, no, sorry, fourth important name we've heard in video games so far on this podcast.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.460
If I had to argue, the first one would be Miyamoto.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.820
Sure. The second one would be Sakamoto.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.340
Oh, actually, you got to throw Iwata in there.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.300
You got to throw Iwata in there.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.220
There's also, like, do we count, like, composers and things like that?

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.000
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I guess that's right, right?

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.320
Yeah, because we already had Nobuo and everything.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.140
Yeah, so, you know, even William Wright did Raid on Bungeling Bay.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:25.620
So, not the...

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.620
Yeah. But definitely one of the bigger echelons.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.500
Yeah, I mean, yeah. It's been a while since we've...

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.980
The video game genre, right?

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.320
Yeah, it's been a while since we've heard a name that is still relevant.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.340
Yeah, I mean, nobody in this room could even tell me who made Major League Baseball, and that was four episodes ago.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.280
Exactly. Correct.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.640
I have no idea for the record.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.040
LJN. LJN did it.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.360
I remember the rainbow. Yeah, Leonard James Nimoy.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.200
Do you use that to help you, like, picture things and all this stuff?

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.120
I'm off the rails now.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.400
Did you accidentally stumble on Leonard Nimoy there?

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.620
It was accidental.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:04:05.060
Anyway, time to talk about Metal Gear, which is like an action stealth game, if you would describe the stealth as such in the game.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.480
I'm sure we'll get into that.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.600
But, yeah, it's actually, like, one of the first stealth games ever.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:15.120
This is. And, I mean, it's still very, very rudimentary.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.880
But this, I mean, what other games are you supposed to avoid all this stuff?

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.300
Right. Like, most of the action games that we play are just, like, kill everybody on the screen.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:33.760
Like, kill everybody. That's why going into it knowing it was a stealth game, at the very beginning, I was, like, still trying to figure out, like, what is a stealth game on the NES?

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.920
Like, they're trying to figure out, like, is it going to be, like, puzzly?

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.840
Like, you've got to avoid these people by doing this?

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.300
Or is it literally just sneaking around?

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.400
Which is kind of what it is.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:41.520
But it surprised me.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:47.000
So just to give those not in the know, this is an overhead action-adventure stealth game.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.840
It revolves around Solid Snake, an operative of the Special Forces unit, Foxhound.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.240
Foxhound, who, and, Sean, you tell me afterwards if this is all, like, still true to the Metal Gear lore or not.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:06.520
Okay. But, so he's an operative for Foxhound who goes on a solo infiltration mission into the fortified state of Outer Heaven to destroy Metal Gear,

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.940
a bipedal walking tank capable of launching nuclear missiles from anywhere in the world,

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:15.880
as well as rescue a number of fellow agents who have been captured by the enemy.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.480
That is all still canon.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:18.860
Cool. That's great.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:24.500
And, you know, throughout the game you collect different weapons, key cards.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.140
Boxes. Yeah, cardboard is important.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:31.800
But the only thing you start off with is a pack of cigarettes.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.880
Yeah. And that's still, like, most games start with a pack of cigarettes.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.040
I love it. Can you actually do anything with the cigarettes?

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.100
You can't actually smoke them.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.480
I tried to.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.280
I was expecting to lose a little bit of life or something.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:45.100
But this is back when cigarettes were cool.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.100
I thought they were going to come in handy or something.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.700
Like, you know, some weird purpose later in the game.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:56.520
What they should have done is be, and again, we're getting into more complex territory.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:08.780
Just say it. In Metal Gear Solid, you could use your cigarettes to show the laser beams that would trip an alarm in, like, some parts of the game.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.820
And there is a part in this game where there are laser beams that could trip alarms.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.240
And I thought that would be where it would be used, but it's not.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:17.940
So let's get into the gameplay.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.560
I, rather than, like, walk through this game, I actually would rather talk about, like, specific categories of the game.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.100
Okay. And we can just cut. You can go anywhere with your examples.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.820
I feel like at this point we've set up what the game is about.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.180
You have to free these agents.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:38.260
You have to try and be stealthy, though you can get into hand-to-hand or weapon combat if necessary.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.600
Yeah. So let's just talk about, overall, the stealth aspect of this game.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.360
Does it actually translate well on an NES title?

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:53.320
No. Yeah, I'd say no, but that doesn't mean that you can't, like, get past it.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.660
Like, there are things you can walk, you could be walking right next to an enemy and they don't notice you.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.820
Yeah. Things like that where it's like, okay, it's hard to make this believable.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.980
And I thought I was going to hate this game right away when I started playing it.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:11.900
But as I played it, I was like, okay, I can still feel like I'm being stealthy in some scenarios.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.440
There's a lot of imagination involved, but, yeah, the actual stealth aspect doesn't really work.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:20.760
I'll start off praising the stealth just for a few reasons.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:29.460
You have to get over the jungle where you start because that is almost like it sets up for the game for you that it's going to be very hard to be stealthy.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:36.520
And, actually, what is stealth in this game if, like, whenever you just show up to a random place, like, they can spot you automatically, right?

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.420
It's like, well, then it's really hard to be stealthy.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:46.820
But I really did like that, you know, I didn't mind enemies not noticing you if you were nearby because they did face specific directions.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.560
Right. If they faced you, they would be able to detect you.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:53.860
But also if you were behind another object, they wouldn't be able to detect you.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:08:01.500
And I thought that was pretty smart for this level of game design, you know, being able to detect whether you're hiding behind something.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:04.340
Later on, you get the cardboard box.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.040
And as long as you don't move, nobody knows anything, the better that you're there.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:16.000
But I also thought, like, some glitches that clearly weren't intended were fun.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:19.400
Like, the cameras, you can just go up against the wall.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:21.120
I don't even think that that...

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.400
Okay, so this game is rife with glitches.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:27.300
Like, it is an entirely broken piece of software.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:30.320
But one thing that I did...

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:34.160
This is a pretty, I think, good headcanon if it wasn't canon.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.180
But I think it's just like, if there was a...

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:44.260
If there's a camera on a wall and you are leaning against that wall, it's entirely possible that you're just not in the shot.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.440
You're just below the camera, yeah.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.640
And I agree with that, and that was my logical reasoning for it, too.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:57.280
I was fine with it. The reason why I know it's a glitch is because in the actual official version that Kojima made on the MSX from Japan, it doesn't work like that.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.940
We'll get all into that complicated history later on, but I think it's important to focus on the NES port.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:04.540
But I like the cameras.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:10.520
The dogs, though, at the beginning of the game, that was like an option where, like, stealth wasn't even available to you.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.420
I was able to...

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.940
And maybe it was just because there was only one dog on this specific screen.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:21.380
But I guess what our point is right now is that, like, the dogs can just detect you automatically if you get close enough.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:26.640
But I was able to, like, if I just moved one square at a time, like, not wake one up, at least.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.220
I was trying to figure it out because they, like, vary, like, to a rhythm.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.940
They lift their heads and then lower them.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.340
And I feel like maybe when they lift their heads, they're, like, listening or they're sniffing or something.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.820
I don't know. But I couldn't really make it work that way.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:42.920
So I don't know if that's really true.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:51.360
But... Yeah. And then there's also, you know, enemy involvement in stealth, too, where they'll fall asleep.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:53.660
Yes. And then you have, like, your moment.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.560
And did anybody ever have anybody wake up on them?

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.960
I was good enough that that didn't happen.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:02.800
Yeah. They wake up sometimes automatically after a few seconds, right?

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.080
Well, they'll say, like, I overslept or something like that.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:07.740
Yeah, I feel asleep. I feel asleep.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.900
Yeah, I feel asleep and then I overslept or something.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.420
But you have a generous amount of time.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.040
But I like those touches.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.780
And they don't happen right away, which helps, too, because you're not entirely sure.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.680
I think early on in the first building, there's also another guard that will just be like,

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:25.200
okay, my shift's up.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:26.660
And then they walk away.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.520
And it's like, it takes about five to ten seconds for that to happen.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.720
So you almost, like, you have to wait out some certain situations.

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.900
Because if you don't, if you're just like, oh, well, I have to fight this guy, you might get into a heap of trouble.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.120
You have to do some recon.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:46.320
It's not just a run and gun game, even though you could treat it that way if you have enough ammo.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:52.840
Right, right. And speaking of, like, when you get caught and stuff, because that's definitely a stealth element, too,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:57.020
that kind of progresses throughout the game what it means to, like, get caught.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:59.600
I don't know how you guys felt about that.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.860
After, like, a while, there's a point where, like, they'll just keep coming.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:10.680
Well, that's another, like, problem with this game is that, and again, comparing it to the MSX version,

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.600
it was always supposed to be global alert if you were spotted.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.940
And if you move to the next screen, you are still getting chased.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.340
Whereas, at least in the early parts of this game, all you have to do is walk off screen and you reset.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:33.080
Right. And actually, that kind of encourages this weird, like, well, I don't really care if they spot me because I see my next exit point.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.720
Defeats the purpose of the game. And then I enter back in and know that I can shoot this guy.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:42.740
And shooting also doesn't, in this version, shooting doesn't alert anybody.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.420
Yeah. No, it does. I know it has a silencer.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:46.220
I mean, if you kill...

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:50.940
If you don't have the silencer, as soon as you fire, everybody looks at you and starts running towards you.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.140
But what if there's only one guy?

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.940
Well, yeah, he will start looking at you and running towards you until he hits your bullet, which is moving slower than him.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:58.880
I didn't always have that experience.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:11:59.940
I had that every time.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:01.200
Interesting. Until I got the silencer.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:09.920
Yes. And I see what you're saying, but I guess what I mean is, in the global system thing, when you shoot a gun and somebody registers it,

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.120
now multiple people from different screens will be coming towards you.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:14.980
In this one, there's no consequence.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:19.020
Even if you do get targeted by that guy, he's going to be dead when the bullet hits him.

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:26.480
No, that's true. For me, it was more if there were five people on your screen already, it'll alert everyone who's already on your screen.

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.380
I don't know if it called more people in or not.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.900
The stealth right now is at 50%.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.860
It's an element that you could appreciate.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:41.080
Yeah. But I mean, flip-flops on being good at certain aspects and then just not really pulling it off.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:48.680
And that kind of makes it a less stressful game overall, though, because you have these little keys you can constantly do to get out of the situation.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:52.180
It cuts away a lot of the tension you would normally feel in a stealth game.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:57.300
And I think this has potential to have a lot of tension in it if it worked the way, Sean, that you just said.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.620
But if it was a global alert.

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:12.820
Yeah, alert. And I feel like, to sum it up for me, it feels like, honestly, most of the elements in this game are really well thought out and could work really well.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:17.880
But it's like somebody took it and it's like every aspect that's really cool is broken.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.840
Everything's broken in some way, but it doesn't work now.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:22.820
And that's how I feel about the stealth.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:29.920
It is really cool, like you said, Mike, that you look at the direction they're facing and it actually has, like, you know, it works in that way.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:34.280
But then it's broken in all the ways that you can cheat it or sometimes it can completely screw you over.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:36.020
That's just out of your control.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.980
I'll have more ways that this game is broken as we go along.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.440
Yes, I'm sure there's no shortage.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:43.380
Yeah, maybe this next one will get you started.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:51.780
This next category, I titled it first Exploration, but I think it's more of just, like, sense of direction or thing to do.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:53.600
Like, your mission as it evolves.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:58.900
This game is not very kind to the person who just continues moving forward.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.900
You do have to do a lot of backtracking.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:04.000
You do have to know your place in the building.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.420
This one would help by drawing a map.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:13.940
It would, but it also, in most cases, like, there is a good sense of place.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.480
Like, most of the screens do look different than each other.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:30.000
Like, yeah, they all have the same palette, but the arrangement and the different elements that are in each screen are recognizable enough that you could create a map of this place in your head to some degree.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:44.020
So, in the very beginning, like, when you finally get through the stupid jungle sequence and into the first building, like, you will probably have the first floor memorized once you see everything on the first floor.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:50.820
And it's a little harder as things go along and you're trying to find doors that were locked before.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:55.320
Like, that is where that really starts to help when you have a map and you can mark locked doors.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:15:04.840
Right, especially because outside of the manual, there's very little clues about where or what to go next.

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:11.000
Yeah. You know, even in the beginning of this game, it's never implied that you should go inside the cars.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.960
And if you do, you have to go in from the back of the car to enter, like, the cargo part.

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:19.960
Whereas, like, most people would probably think, well, if I could go in these, it would be through the front door.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:24.920
There is a line in the manual that says that there are things in the trucks.

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:26.540
Right, no, totally. And that's what I'm saying.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.500
You know, the game didn't miss that.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:36.340
And it is, a lot of stuff is actually covered in the manual in terms of, like, figuring out, you know, a process of elimination on what to do next.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:40.340
I just think that with a game that had a transceiver.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:45.820
Yeah. You know, that, like, the fact that it doesn't help you kind of figure out what you have to do next.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:50.160
I mean, you can go all the way to the first gas chamber, right?

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:51.640
Yeah. Without your gas mask.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:53.380
And only in that room.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.360
I forgot to tell you.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:00.520
As you're dying, he finally says, like, hey, you know, I should have mentioned this, but you should go get a gas mask.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:04.560
But now it's, like, it's up to you now to figure out, like, well, I never saw that thing.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:08.360
Where the heck is that? Okay, so the other things that happen in this game, like, it does tell you.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:09.580
It just, it's hidden.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:18.920
So when you're walking around and, like, your secondary objective is to rescue hostages, aside from, like, the main hostage, like, Gray Fox.

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:23.620
And when you talk to them, like, sometimes they'll just say, like, oh, what a relief.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.420
I'm so glad I get to leave now.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.820
But others will say, what a relief.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:29.500
I'm so glad I get to leave now.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:34.480
Also, did you know that there's a gas mask in this floor, on this side of the place?

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.240
And, like, just really random things.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.500
And there are other times where it's, like, you should talk to Schneider.

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:43.240
And Schneider's on this channel.

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.160
And so you do end up fiddling around with the transceiver.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.260
And you end up finding Schneider.

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:51.560
And he's, like, this is where this stuff is.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:53.600
So, I mean, like, it's all there.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:17:00.280
Yeah, I would say, like, granted, it wasn't perfect in the way that it, like, guides you and directs you into where you've got to go.

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:05.180
But I kind of felt this little, like, satisfaction of exploring and kind of piecing it together.

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:09.180
Like, oh, okay, so I'm going to need this now because there's gas here or whatever it is.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:12.540
You know, like, piecing it together and being like, oh, where was that door?

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:13.420
Now I got this card key.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:14.620
I go back in there and find something.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:19.440
Like, oh, this will help me get through the, I don't know, the area where you've got to blow up the electrical floor.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:23.180
And, you know, everything kind of comes together in an organic-ish.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:28.300
Yeah, I'm not trying to compare this to, like, Metroid levels of, like, where the heck am I?

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:30.540
And then I go all this way for nothing, right?

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:31.920
It's not like that.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:41.580
I think what's weirder, though, is that if, and this isn't even true on the MSX version, but if enemies, like, perma-died, right?

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:49.240
Like, when you took care of them, it might make exploration a little more, like, it might make it more enjoyable.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:57.440
Because sometimes, especially on this NES version, when you backtrack, you go through doors where guards are standing right on that door.

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:09.640
Yeah, yeah. One of the biggest technical issues with this game is that, like, if it was on a timer, maybe, if it was, like, every 40 seconds to a minute, everything respawns.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:19.980
Yeah, but in this game, if you just open your menu, if you go to the transceiver, if you go and change your weapon, everything in that room that you're in respawns.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:26.700
And it just breaks the entire experience, because now you have to expend health.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:28.060
Now you have to expend ammunition.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:30.140
Now you have to run like a chicken.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:36.100
Like, it really breaks whatever immersion that they've created through this stealth system.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:41.140
And I don't think that that—so that was also present in the MSX system?

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:47.020
In the MSX version, when you go back to a—it doesn't do the reset thing when you go to menus and stuff.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:54.040
But when you go back, even to the stage you were just at, it has, you know, it has them there.

00:18:54.160 --> 00:19:03.620
And that's the same thing that's true of the global alert system is, like, the enemies that pour into the screen from other areas that they can enter from, they're not the enemies from the other screen.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.320
Yeah, yeah, yeah. It's not actually tracking the whole building.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:07.740
That would be quite a feat.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:16.620
Yeah. But I actually—I'm okay with, like, the timer thing, you know, because it makes sense that, like, they would have more guards that would be able to, like—

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:17.700
Eventually, yeah. Yeah, check this out.

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:20.980
But then also, at the same time, wouldn't they be like, hey, a bunch of dead bodies over here.

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:22.700
Maybe we should start, like, looking for a guy.

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.920
And you start having to make Metal Gear Solid out of it.

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:25.920
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:39.940
One of the sequences that you have to go to—like, we were going back to the gas mask thing.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:43.820
Mm-hmm. You have—like, it's just the entire inventory system.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:47.800
Like, the controls are all weird on each step.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:51.760
Um, then you have to be carrying the card key.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:56.360
That must be your primary item whenever you're going into a door that needs a card key.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:01.440
Yeah, you have to select—you have to go in and select the card—the specific card key out of, like, 17 of them.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:03.720
So you're, like, guess—no, aren't there more than that?

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.540
There's eight or nine. There's eight, yeah.

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:12.280
Um, and then you have to immediately, in this gas-filled room, you're taking damage, like, from the first frame.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:14.060
And then you have to change into your gas mask.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:17.800
And then you have to walk to the other door and take out your card key again.

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:19.560
And then you're immediately taking damage again.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:27.060
And, like, I—maybe if they just had a—this is a nitpick to an extent, but maybe if they had a separate slot for the card key.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:32.340
Yeah. Or if you just, like, if your card key was just something that you didn't have to select.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:39.980
And once you got—like, here's the really annoying part about the card key system for me, was once you get card key number two, right?

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:42.300
Now, all of a sudden, you have more than one card key to manage.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:47.640
The card key number two doesn't have, like, level two access to, like, one and two.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:54.240
Right, yeah, yeah. If you go to a door now and you have, let's say, key cards one through eight, and for some reason—I really don't think this is going to happen.

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:57.440
I think once you get to, like, key card eight, you really only have to worry about seven and eight.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:20:59.200
Yeah. But let's just say, for the record—

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:00.140
But even one through four, isn't it?

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:05.080
But, yeah, exactly. If you had to, like, try to guess, like, oh, which door is it this time?

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:06.060
Is it level five?

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:11.720
Like, you have to select through, select equipment, select the key card.

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:14.580
It's a lot of menuing for nothing.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:25.860
I think that people that might defend this system would say that, like, if you're just going to simplify it down to an upgraded key card, then that removes some, like, puzzle elements or challenge.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:27.200
But you still have to find the key card.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:31.020
It's really just adding busy work in your inventory system.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:33.480
Like, it really—it should just be you have the key card.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:34.760
You now have access to all these doors.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:35.940
You don't have to select anything.

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:37.000
You have the next key card.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:38.220
All right, you have access to all these, too.

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:39.500
Like, it shouldn't even be a selectable item.

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:46.780
But, again, this is also, like, a new—not a new idea, but it's an iteration on boss keys.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:54.400
And so I can kind of—like, from the time that this was made, I can forgive this mistake.

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:56.740
Me, too. But it's still very annoying.

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:59.700
Yeah, I mean, they could have went even more aggressive on this if you think about it.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:03.260
You might have had to actually, like, go to the menu to reload.

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:11.260
Yeah, that's true. You know, like, thankfully, it's like when you get your ammunition, you can exit the room, go back, and it's still there and just stock up as much as you want.

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:12.600
But that's also a problem.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:17.920
Like, if you pick—like, other problems with this whole inventory system crop up that are bugs.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:22.640
Like, if you pick up ammunition before you pick up the gun, that ammunition goes nowhere.

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:24.140
That happened to me. I was so mad.

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:25.600
Then I was like, where was that ammo again?

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:26.620
I don't know. I don't remember.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:36.260
You have to go pick up more ammo now. And then when you—if you're in a room that has an item, like rations or ammo, all you have to do is go into the menu and come out, and there's the ammo.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:37.860
Right. You don't even have to leave the room.

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:42.200
You have to leave the room. Every time you find ammo, you can very easily just max out your ammo.

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:44.780
Which is—I mean, of course, I did that every time also.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:53.620
But it is kind of sad because I feel like with, you know, you get 20—you find ammo with how scarcely you do find it, and you get, what, 20 rounds?

00:22:53.840 --> 00:23:03.820
Yeah. Then it is kind of this ammo management kind of level of the game that we're kind of missing out on because you don't really have to do that because it's really easy to just get as much ammo as you need.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:06.040
I don't know if it's like a RAM issue or—

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:12.700
Yeah. But a lot of things could have been vastly improved if this game had a longer memory.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:17.420
Yeah. Like, you just, like, remembered enemies dying, remembered—I don't know.

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:21.160
Yeah, because, no, it's all exploitations that are too easy to ignore.

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:25.600
Right. Right? Yeah. Like, you almost, at that point, you could do some of these things on accident.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:30.200
Yeah. You could be like, oh, well, my health is low, so I'm going to eat the rations right now.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:33.100
And then you exit out of the menu, and it's there in front of you.

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:34.620
Yeah. And you're like, hey, that's exactly what I need.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:35.660
That's how I discovered it.

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.400
Yeah. And that's what happened to me, too.

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:45.620
And before I knew about that, I was—I don't know why I'm hung up on this, but I did think it was really cool when I was playing that, like, every time I would fire the gun, I would think about it.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:47.500
I'd be like, wow, do I really want to waste this bullet?

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:49.180
Like, it had some weight to it.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:52.780
And then afterwards, I was like, I guess if I get caught, I can just shoot everyone.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:53.840
Like, it's not a deal.

00:23:53.840 --> 00:24:00.660
Right, right, right. Right. And we'll get to combat, but the next thing I wanted to talk about was finding the agents.

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:15.220
Okay. And the reason specifically, if I can make a topic starter here, it's because this could have been an added element of the game that could have been a lot cooler if they were not—if they didn't feel like such a natural progression part of the game.

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:21.620
They're only hidden behind door key cards that if you're playing this game correctly, you're checking everything anyway.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:28.380
Right? Yeah. You don't know when the next, like, important item is going to be, like, you know, the next weapon you find is going to be behind one of those doors.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.820
So you're checking for everything.

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:41.420
I almost wish that the agents required, like, some kind of—not puzzle solving, because I understand that would be very hard to do in this overhead aspect, but some kind of, like, extra—additional searching.

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:46.580
I liken it to, in the first building, there's that rolling log.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:51.180
Yeah. Right? And you go to one side, you've got to get the plastic explosives.

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:56.220
Then you go down again, and then you let the rolling log come by, and then you exit out the other way.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:59.320
Little things like that, or the pitfalls, you know?

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:09.060
Having things like that make it perilous to get to the agent would have been more rewarding than just, well, I'm going to see what's behind this door because I have to check every single thing.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:10.560
Oh, it's an agent. Great.

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:12.040
I think then you would—

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:21.120
I'm going to free them. You would need to also—I know that you get, like, a class upgrade for every, what, five agents you get or something?

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:25.940
Yeah, I don't know how it happens per agents, but that class thing is important.

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:35.900
Yeah. But I think you would also need more of a—because you can go through this game without getting most of them, if you so chose to.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:47.220
I think you might need a little bit more of a carrot to—if you're going to add extra challenge to get to these guys, I think you need more of a reward for getting to them.

00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:53.920
Well, I think the rank is actually really important, though, because if you're, like, rank four, you can hold so much more ammunition.

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:57.380
You can—you have double the health bar than what you start with at rank one.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.800
I think— All right, I'll concede that.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:06.180
That's actually a really good incentive because that, like, makes you feel stronger as a player.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:07.540
All right, I concede that.

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:15.340
Yeah. You know, it reminds me of in Legend of Zelda how, like, you can beat Ganon with whatever you have at your disposal, right?

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:16.520
You really only need the sword.

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:17.540
You just have to get to the room.

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:25.620
But no one's stopping you from picking up the white tunic, you know, or the—it's not the master sword in that game, but the more powerful sword.

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.640
Like, you can get those things, and when you get them, you feel better as a player.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:29.540
It's the same thing with the agents.

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:35.780
The thing I don't like about the agents is that they're—there's not really, like, a discovery system to them.

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:39.220
They're just there, and so it feels natural that you're going to progress with them.

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:43.360
It almost feels like it's just punishing people who don't check every door.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:46.320
True. But I feel like in this game—

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:50.400
And they do also give you, like, a bit of information.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:55.400
Right. In this game, as a stealth game, it's almost like you want them to check everything, you know?

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:57.420
Like, you want to encourage that kind of behavior.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:26:59.560
So maybe it is, maybe it isn't.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.100
Joe, any thoughts on that? I'm in.

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:02.100
I agree. Okay.

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:04.640
Cool. All right.

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:08.720
So now let's talk about the combat of the game.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:16.900
And this includes all the weapons, so if anybody wants to talk about some cool weapons or anything, I'm totally down to talk about those things as well.

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:23.380
But just, you know, now that you have just your fist to start, how do you feel with just your fist?

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:28.480
I mean, I feel vulnerable with just my fist, especially around the dogs.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:37.360
But thankfully, as soon as you get into the building, if you check another truck, you're going to find the gun, and you're going to find some ammo.

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:40.620
And then you just feel a lot more comfortable.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:48.820
And I feel like it does a good job of, because if you sneak up on someone and use your fist, you win.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.200
Like, it's really hard to lose if you get the first hit in.

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:59.860
But then if you get caught and a couple people come over to you, you might be in some trouble, which is a good way of kind of right off the bat rewarding stealth.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:04.040
And, like, no one had really played stealth games before, I assume, back then.

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:17.280
So it was a good way of kind of showing people right away, like, you want to stay hidden in this game, rather than just do what you probably do in most other games you played at that point, which is just go all in.

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:25.160
Yeah, having the gun alert, I think, is a better design decision.

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:30.300
We got into the ammo thing already of, like, when you don't have a lot of ammo, it is a tense situation.

00:28:30.940 --> 00:28:40.980
But not, you know, I actually appreciate that they made it that it's one shot kills anybody for the most part, other than the guys later on who have, like, they look like they have body armor on or something.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:41.920
So that makes sense.

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:44.660
But I like that they have the one hit kill thing.

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:55.820
But at the same time, when you're being, like, rushed by five or six guys, and you just, yeah, and they go in a single file, and you just shoot them down the line, it's like, maybe that's how it would happen in real life.

00:28:55.820 --> 00:29:00.900
But I have to imagine that a terrorist organization would be a little more tactical.

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:02.360
Maybe they wouldn't be just running at you.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:03.640
They'd be using their guns.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:04.680
Right, right, right. Exactly.

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:06.760
Sometimes. Sometimes they use their guns.

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:09.220
In fact, there's one guy early on in the jungle.

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:10.660
Yeah, he's very trigger happy.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:17.960
Yeah, when you go into the van to get the first key card, he automatically spots you.

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:24.240
It's like the immediate, I don't know what they were trying to teach you there, but you have to, like, just get up very close to him and punch him.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:26.320
Yeah. And knock him out.

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:38.180
And I don't know about you guys, but, like, replay throughs of this, sometimes I just would, like, fuck up the first couple of punches, and then he would, like, just get out of my way, start shooting at me.

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:39.520
Then I'd go run towards him.

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:41.260
He'd move in a different place. I was like, who is this guy?

00:29:41.320 --> 00:29:42.300
He should be in charge.

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:45.440
He should be in charge of this organization.

00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:46.740
He knows what's good.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:48.760
So you get the handgun.

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:51.600
You have the silencer. You got a couple more thoughts?

00:29:51.600 --> 00:30:09.280
Well, I got a thought on what you said about them running at you single file and being kind of easy to take out once you get caught, which, again, harkens back to the global alert system where that would at least – even though everyone's easy to take out, there's a consequence to the fact that you're using your gun and you're making a commotion.

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:12.020
That would, I think, be the, like, remedy there.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:22.000
I don't think even – I think the fact that we keep coming back to this isn't just that we have the foreknowledge of these games end up having global alert.

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:33.760
Right, right. It's that even if I were playing this for the first time in 1987 or 1988, I would expect the people 20 meters away to also hear this.

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:41.660
Right, yeah. So, I mean, I think it's very – I think it's deserved criticism to keep bringing it up.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:45.500
So we also have the plastic explosives and the landmines.

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:48.760
Not entirely, like –

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:49.740
I didn't use them very often.

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:50.800
Yeah, not entirely useful.

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:56.480
I think the landmines are good in certain routes for the enemies.

00:30:56.480 --> 00:31:03.860
Like, if you see a guy who's walking around in, like, a square or something and you don't want to try to just, like, sneak around him, you can leave it.

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:05.960
Yeah, also, like, a couple boss fights.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:11.200
Yeah. Yeah, there were a few times where I was being chased and I would drop him and at first I was like, this is great.

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.900
I'm being able to kill these people without having to turn around and fight them and then I can get off the screen.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:17.160
Then I realized, like, oh, but I can just get off the screen.

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:19.800
Just get off the screen. Which brings me back to the global alert system.

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:21.920
They're going to bring up after every point that I make.

00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:27.960
Yeah, yeah, yeah. But the plastic explosives, they do come into use at the very end of the game against the supercomputer.

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:32.920
You have to plant 16 of them against the supercomputer.

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.420
And it's just like –

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:38.660
I think you can also just use the rocket launcher, though.

00:31:39.620 --> 00:31:40.820
Hmm. I wonder.

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:45.780
Maybe. But maybe you would need, like, a lot more rockets.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:49.220
I mean, I – yeah, but again, it's ammunition that just –

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:50.380
Right, that you replenish.

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:55.880
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah, yeah. For some reason, I remember, like, I think someone in the game tells you about the 16 thing.

00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:57.740
I don't recall that.

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:02.520
Yeah. But also, if you don't want to do that, I thought you were going to hint to the other thing.

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:06.980
That is, if you enter the room with the supercomputer at the end of the game and you immediately,

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:12.260
as you're up against the door, just hit the right button on the D-pad, you'll get warped to the final boss.

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:16.960
It'll just – it's just a mistake in the game that'll just take you right to the final boss

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:21.360
and you won't have to worry about the supercomputer or escaping the room or anything like that.

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:22.500
You're totally fine.

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:24.080
You go right to the final boss.

00:32:24.220 --> 00:32:26.960
So if you don't have 16 explosives on you, maybe try the right button.

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:30.880
Because you may defeat the final boss, but the supercomputer is still at large.

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:32.700
That's true. And the supercomputer –

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:35.080
You can't really beat the game. But does a supercomputer have Wi-Fi?

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:37.840
No. Okay. Then I don't think there's a much of a problem.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.360
Yeah. It's just contained, you know, pure evil or whatever.

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:42.780
Yeah. He's just seething.

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:46.060
Right. And then – oh, this one was cool.

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:47.480
The remote-guided missiles.

00:32:47.780 --> 00:32:49.140
Yeah. That was unexpected, right?

00:32:49.140 --> 00:32:50.220
Yeah, I did not expect that at all.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:52.600
I remember that, again, from Metal Gear Solid.

00:32:53.700 --> 00:33:02.200
I just think it's funny that, you know, in a game like this, you can launch something and then take control of that missile.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:05.820
It's weird, like, seeing the character that you've been controlling throughout the game.

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:08.420
All of a sudden, now you're in charge of this missile.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:15.220
It would be like if in, you know, in Super Mario Brothers, you just all of a sudden shoot the fireball, and it's like now you have control over the fireball.

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:21.080
Or, like, if you're in, like, Super Smash Brothers and you shoot, like, a rocket and you can control it there.

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:23.180
Oh, gotcha. But you can do that, right?

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:26.200
Oh, you're right. And you only have Solid Snake, though.

00:33:26.680 --> 00:33:28.980
Oh, you're right. Ah, no, no, no.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:30.380
You can do it as Samus 2.

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:33.420
Oh. Sorry. Depends on the type of missile you shoot, though.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:40.140
She has one that's the super missile that you can't control, and then the missile, the regular missile that you can't control.

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:45.620
Yeah, missiles. But this one was actually used in a cool place early on in the game, the remote-guided missile.

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:47.460
Oh, yeah, to turn the switch off.

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:49.480
Yeah, you have to, like, shoot the circuit breaker.

00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:54.760
Yeah. And then it turns off the, what I'm going to guess is, like, the electric voltage floor.

00:33:55.060 --> 00:33:57.180
Yeah. Which is just like, is nobody else walking there?

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:59.140
Right. Does nobody go through that section?

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:00.240
Well, they're not patrolling there.

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:04.480
It's funny, though. Do, like, military bases have, like, little traps like that?

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:06.420
Like, electrified floors and stuff?

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:07.460
Military organizations, no.

00:34:07.820 --> 00:34:09.400
Terrorist organizations, yes.

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:22.540
I did also appreciate, like, the, like, just cute little scripting things of, like, if you walk into one of these areas that has an electrified floor and there's another, like, guard there, he won't try to attack you.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:27.700
He will run to the switch to electrocute you that way.

00:34:27.700 --> 00:34:33.660
I'm like, you know, a lot of the AI in this game is trash, but stuff like that, you know, adds a little bit of flavor.

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:36.620
Yeah. And it's clearly just programmed to do that.

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:38.960
Yeah. But, like, hey, you know, it is something.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:48.540
Yeah. Then you also have the grenade launcher and the submachine gun, but both of those are just kind of, like, increased firepower to take on the heavier enemies of the game.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:50.520
Yeah. Which is fine.

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:51.920
You definitely need stuff like that.

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:58.880
I think it would be kind of immersion breaking if I'm shooting a handgun at, like, what is the, is there a helicopter in this one?

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:00.420
No, that's the original version.

00:35:00.840 --> 00:35:03.400
You have twin gunner or double gunner.

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:05.080
Gotcha. Just two guys on turrets.

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:06.440
Got it. Okay. Yeah.

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:08.420
So at least there's some reason to it.

00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:17.620
Mm-hmm. And then the rocket launcher, which you'll definitely need because it's the only way to take down the final boss of the game, Big Boss himself.

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.120
Yeah. And is that a twist?

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:21.640
That is a twist.

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:27.640
Yeah. It surprised me. So this is something that I've been wanting to get into.

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:37.040
And while this is still, again, very basic, I really appreciate how complex this world is.

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:45.400
It's, like, in most of the games that we've played so far, you have this very basic reason to be playing this game.

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:51.540
Whether it's digging up blocks and burying people or shooting or throwing fireballs at little Goombas.

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:56.120
Whereas in this one, like, the transceiver tells a story.

00:35:57.100 --> 00:36:06.000
And later in the game, not only does it break the fourth wall and try and get you to, like, and try to throw you off the tail that way.

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:15.440
But there is a twist ending with this whole, sorry for the spoilers, this whole, your superior Big Boss is the enemy.

00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:19.460
And, yeah, it's really basic by today's standards.

00:36:19.660 --> 00:36:23.860
But, like, this is the biggest story we've played so far.

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:30.480
And I feel like, I mean, I can't speak for you guys, but I feel like even in today, knowing today's standards, this shocked me.

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:37.520
Because thinking of it as an NES game, I did not expect there to be a plot twist that was that, like, unsuspected.

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:40.460
This is the biggest twist on this show since Double Dragon.

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:44.480
Joe, you weren't here for that episode.

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:56.400
But at the end of the arcade version, not the NES version, when you're playing co-op, after you defeat Willy, you have to fight each other for who gets the rights to the girl.

00:36:56.400 --> 00:36:57.720
Oh, I knew that. I didn't know that.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:04.160
We actually had a listener write into the show last week, Diego.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:07.840
He was talking about Double Dragon and his memories with it.

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:12.880
And he brought up this funny one that I know has nothing to do with Metal Gear, but we're going to talk about it real fast because it made me laugh.

00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:20.420
He said, at the end of Double Dragon for Master System, you have to fight each other to decide who keeps the girl or something like that.

00:37:20.620 --> 00:37:26.160
My brother suggested we both jump at the pit, so both die to see what happens.

00:37:26.620 --> 00:37:30.000
He said on three, we both jump, but only I jumped.

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:35.220
And I think that's so funny that he has, like, that memory forever now.

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:37.480
Now I want to know what happens.

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:39.000
If they both jump at the same time, right?

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:42.240
Don't fall for it. If you're ever playing Double Dragon with Sean, don't fall for it.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:44.040
If you ever find yourself in an arcade with Sean.

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:47.380
Going back to Metal Gear, though.

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:53.160
Because, I mean, I say that the story is, you know, a bit more complex than what we played.

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:56.020
But I guess technically Rambo tells the story of Rambo.

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:57.820
I was just thinking about Rambo when you were mentioning it.

00:37:57.820 --> 00:38:06.840
But when I think about the storytelling of Rambo the game, it's more like a toddler, like, vaguely recalling the movie that his dad was watching.

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:11.220
And it's like, and then he just talks to the stranger at the dock.

00:38:11.220 --> 00:38:12.620
And then there was a snake everywhere.

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:14.560
You do a good toddler.

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:21.520
And now this game is actually telling a mostly coherent story with a little bit of poor translation thrown in.

00:38:21.520 --> 00:38:22.860
Yeah, just bad English is the problem.

00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:25.700
That's all. But you understand at least what they're getting at.

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:27.780
Yeah. You know? I feel asleep is fine.

00:38:29.660 --> 00:38:31.880
Yeah. So thank you for that, Sean.

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:34.580
I'm not going to fight you on the story part here.

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:36.220
I thought it was pretty immersive.

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:40.980
I think it is weird that Big Boss can only be defeated by a rocket launcher.

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:43.400
Yeah. That just seems strange.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:44.300
Well, he's big.

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:46.180
Yeah. And he's the boss.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:47.580
Yeah. How big is he?

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.600
He's pretty boss. But it's just like, you know, I don't know.

00:38:49.940 --> 00:38:54.720
I'm not saying give him the handgun, but if I have the grenade launcher, it should work the same, right?

00:38:54.860 --> 00:38:57.860
But only the rocket launcher will do damage to Big Boss.

00:38:58.880 --> 00:39:03.280
And the select glitch is in total effect here.

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:07.660
So if you want, you can always just select him back to reset in his place.

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:12.880
All of the bosses can be menued back to their original spot.

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:14.460
I didn't know that.

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:16.740
Do they gain their health back?

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:18.820
I mean, I don't really...

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:21.120
In all the mini bosses, I didn't even fight him.

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:22.260
I just walked around them.

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:24.420
So I can't really...

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:25.600
I can't go into that detail.

00:39:25.900 --> 00:39:26.880
Yeah. I think...

00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:29.600
I can't speak for whether he does that or not.

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:31.860
But you don't even need to know if it resets the health or not.

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:34.740
Because actually, there's an even bigger exploit that was...

00:39:34.740 --> 00:39:36.720
Whether or not it was programmed in the game is anybody's guess.

00:39:36.900 --> 00:39:41.380
But if you just go to the top right corner, Big Boss will just stay in the lower left...

00:39:41.380 --> 00:39:42.240
Lower right corner.

00:39:42.380 --> 00:39:45.920
And he'll just stand there right at you so you can just aim the rockets right at him.

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:49.700
It's unbelievable. But it happens every time without failing.

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:51.080
It's used in speed runs and everything.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:52.260
It's just known.

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:55.080
It's like, yep, that Big Boss, he's really good until you put him in a corner.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:03.340
All right. That's mostly what I had to say in terms of topics.

00:40:03.740 --> 00:40:07.840
Oh, the low health at the start of the game.

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:11.060
It made you appreciate it when you got more health, right?

00:40:11.180 --> 00:40:14.760
Absolutely, yeah. I actually liked the idea of...

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:18.980
It was very similar to in Legend of Zelda when you get more hearts and everything.

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:20.440
You feel like a stronger player.

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:24.220
So I did notice that I was dying a lot early on.

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:32.720
But in the later half of the game, unless you're being careless, you can think about your situations and plan things out.

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:38.080
And I appreciated being able to do that in a game as opposed to in most NES games that we have.

00:40:38.200 --> 00:40:45.260
You have to always be on or at least the best you can do is just pause and try to think about what might come next.

00:40:45.260 --> 00:40:49.980
In this game, you can actually check your screen or use the binoculars, which I would never recommend.

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:54.000
If you're in a place that has enemies in it originally.

00:40:54.420 --> 00:40:56.780
Yeah. You can use the binoculars, see what's ahead of you.

00:40:56.860 --> 00:41:04.840
It feels like there's maybe a very small baseline of strategy added to this game that feels good.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:07.220
Yeah. So I appreciated that.

00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:08.640
Thank you.

00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:43.960
The passcode system.

00:41:45.060 --> 00:41:46.160
Okay. It's long.

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.720
It's very long. So there's a lot of things going on there.

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:53.860
I mean, I guess it is saving a good amount of information.

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:56.060
Yes. Especially with all the items and everything you have.

00:41:56.400 --> 00:42:00.620
Yeah. And the ammo for each or the amount of each item.

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:02.780
Yeah. So, I mean, I get it.

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:07.520
I'm not really, it's not really going to be a make or break for me if the passwords are long.

00:42:07.700 --> 00:42:12.940
Yeah. And obviously they weren't going to put like save slots into it because they weren't, Konami

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:15.320
was not going to pay for the batteries to do that.

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:19.260
Yeah. So I'm fine with the password system as it is.

00:42:19.460 --> 00:42:21.700
Apparently there are some fun passwords you could put in there though.

00:42:21.860 --> 00:42:24.740
Yeah. Are you talking about the F me one?

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:34.100
Yeah. If you put in F-U-C-K me and then fill the remaining spaces with one, like one, one,

00:42:38.440 --> 00:42:42.560
the player to the final battle, but with no equipment.

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:45.720
Truly telling you to go F yourself.

00:42:45.820 --> 00:42:50.420
That's crazy actually that somebody planned that and put that into this game.

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:51.420
It's very cute. I love that.

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:54.920
There's also some other things you can do though too with the Metal Gear passwords.

00:42:57.460 --> 00:42:58.520
Yeah.

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:13.920
with Bailey, whatever, Justin Bailey.

00:43:14.460 --> 00:43:15.960
Oh. You put his name in,

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:20.020
and then you can play as Samus without her clothes on.

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:22.140
Ah. In her swimsuit.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:24.060
Zero? Oh, okay. Not Zero Suit.

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:24.980
That didn't exist yet.

00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:27.200
That was in Zero Mission.

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:29.600
Whatever. I don't care.

00:43:29.840 --> 00:43:31.780
All right. Hey, I think you do care, actually.

00:43:31.900 --> 00:43:34.800
I think you're pretty into Samus.

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:37.800
He's like, do I say that on the podcast?

00:43:38.100 --> 00:43:44.120
I do. The NES version of Metal Gear sold over 1 million copies.

00:43:44.180 --> 00:43:45.660
That's a huge success.

00:43:46.020 --> 00:43:49.860
Yeah. But I need to talk to you guys about the MSX version

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:51.520
because I did play it.

00:43:51.640 --> 00:43:53.440
I would love to talk about it.

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:54.700
You did play it? I didn't.

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:55.900
That's why I want to talk about it

00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:56.920
because I watched some videos on it,

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:58.260
and I really want to play it.

00:43:58.260 --> 00:43:59.960
It is a much better game.

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:00.900
It looks like it.

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:04.000
It is so good. It seems like it's everything I like about this game

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:06.520
without all the things I don't like about this game.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:09.680
There are better story beats if that's what you're looking for.

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:13.700
There's better stealth mechanics if that's what you're looking for.

00:44:14.280 --> 00:44:16.100
Yep. Better graphics.

00:44:16.420 --> 00:44:19.080
Yeah, better graphics, too, and also better sound.

00:44:19.680 --> 00:44:24.380
The music is throughout the entire game.

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:27.260
I can't remember now. Yeah, there's music pretty much throughout.

00:44:27.320 --> 00:44:28.920
It's the same two tracks.

00:44:29.260 --> 00:44:30.720
Right, right. That's fine. It's the same thing there, too.

00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:32.820
You don't start in the jungle, though.

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:35.580
The jungle was just a made-up thing on the NES version

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:38.160
because you know what else was weird about that jungle in the NES version?

00:44:38.380 --> 00:44:42.020
You parachute after three other guys parachute,

00:44:42.200 --> 00:44:44.100
and we never hear from them again.

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:45.720
They all got caught in the trees.

00:44:45.820 --> 00:44:46.800
They were unrelated.

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:48.000
They were not part of your mission.

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:50.620
They were just parachuting in there for other reasons.

00:44:50.980 --> 00:44:51.880
They were just tourist parachutists in South Africa.

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:54.420
I'm going with Sean's idea.

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:58.580
They landed in the trees, and it's up to Rambo to go find them.

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:01.080
That's somebody else's problem.

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:04.000
Yes, I played through the MSX version.

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:07.740
The map system is also a little different.

00:45:08.200 --> 00:45:10.920
It's not the same exact one-for-one as the NES.

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:12.220
It was a maze. Yeah.

00:45:13.460 --> 00:45:18.200
The NES took some liberties on how to redesign the game,

00:45:18.220 --> 00:45:22.420
and interestingly enough, Kojima had no say in the NES version of the game.

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:24.120
He wasn't involved at all in the NES version.

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:26.520
He's completely disowned this version of the game.

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:28.040
I was reading about that, yeah.

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:33.620
Yeah, which I would expect out of somebody as protective of their work as Kojima.

