I shouldn't say the biggest, but, like, it's a pretty big experience.

00:59:01.520 --> 00:59:04.960
Yeah. Like, I feel like going back to it, you're like, okay, where was I again?

00:59:05.020 --> 00:59:06.480
What information do I have?

00:59:06.560 --> 00:59:08.020
You know, it's pretty immersive.

00:59:10.960 --> 00:59:13.180
Yes, the problem is that it's broken.

00:59:14.060 --> 00:59:17.780
And I keep having this, like, memory of when I was playing Skyrim.

00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:20.720
I was playing Skyrim on the PS3 when it first came out.

00:59:20.880 --> 00:59:21.900
I know, bear with me here.

00:59:22.140 --> 00:59:26.600
Okay. And there was a glitch that I didn't realize had been patched.

00:59:26.660 --> 00:59:29.960
And the glitch was that if you touch water, your game crashes.

00:59:31.460 --> 00:59:37.600
I didn't know that. And I still enjoyed playing Skyrim that way, but it was really, really annoying.

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:41.140
Until I realized it was patched out and I fixed it.

00:59:41.260 --> 00:59:43.300
But that's kind of how I feel when I'm playing this.

00:59:43.400 --> 00:59:45.620
Like, wow, this is such a cool game, but nothing works.

00:59:46.760 --> 00:59:54.700
So that is the biggest reason why I, as much as I want to, I also can't put it on the Essential

00:59:54.700 --> 01:00:00.280
Games list, but I can't speak to the MSX version, but I really want to play that.

01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:04.380
And I feel like maybe if I played that, that one could get on the Essential Games list.

01:00:04.560 --> 01:00:05.960
The MSX Essential Games list.

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:07.420
Yes, when we get to that.

01:00:07.420 --> 01:00:09.140
Right, right, right. Whenever, somehow.

01:00:10.060 --> 01:00:12.560
All right. Here's what I have to say about all this.

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:14.660
Forget that there's another version.

01:00:15.300 --> 01:00:21.100
Forget that this game is so well-contained and tells a great story.

01:00:21.100 --> 01:00:30.080
The biggest problem I have with this game is that the flaws that are in this game, for me

01:00:30.080 --> 01:00:34.480
personally, it's fun despite all of these flaws, right?

01:00:34.660 --> 01:00:37.020
Yeah. It's so much fun to actually play this game.

01:00:37.240 --> 01:00:42.120
But every single time something happens that you don't want to happen, you kind of laugh

01:00:42.120 --> 01:00:44.420
it off. And you're like, oh, this game would be a lot better if that didn't happen.

01:00:45.280 --> 01:00:46.200
I'll keep playing.

01:00:47.460 --> 01:00:49.220
But man, I wish that didn't happen just now.

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:50.800
And you just keep going and going.

01:00:50.800 --> 01:00:55.560
And then eventually, you might die and get reset back to a point that's just a little

01:00:55.560 --> 01:00:56.740
too far for you for that day.

01:00:56.740 --> 01:01:00.420
So you just write down the password or whatever and kid to yourself that you're going to go

01:01:00.420 --> 01:01:01.400
back. You know?

01:01:01.520 --> 01:01:03.000
I'm not speaking for personal experience here.

01:01:04.640 --> 01:01:06.600
But that's how I felt about this game overall.

01:01:06.600 --> 01:01:09.940
So it can't go onto the essential games list for me.

01:01:10.080 --> 01:01:13.360
But I do have to call out that it's fun.

01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:21.440
Especially I feel like this game is more interesting now than it was maybe when it first came out.

01:01:21.560 --> 01:01:25.700
It was maybe too obtuse back then.

01:01:25.960 --> 01:01:27.560
It's so innovative.

01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:35.420
And it's aspiring to be something so much bigger than we've seen recently that you have

01:01:35.420 --> 01:01:36.300
to give it credit.

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:37.140
What is it?

01:01:37.240 --> 01:01:40.740
Like Michael J. Fox in Back to the Future when he's like, you know, you guys might not

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:42.840
like this, but your kids are going to love it.

01:01:43.700 --> 01:01:45.320
It's like, that's how I feel about this.

01:01:45.400 --> 01:01:49.120
I feel like it could actually be appreciated more now, especially with everything we know

01:01:49.120 --> 01:01:55.360
about Kojima. Like being able to like, imagine if you could go back to one of Spielberg's like earliest

01:01:55.360 --> 01:01:59.140
films that like you don't even know about and be like, oh wow, this was the inspiration

01:01:59.140 --> 01:02:00.240
for so much of his stuff.

01:02:00.380 --> 01:02:03.920
You can't because you'd watch Duel and be like, oh, that's weird.

01:02:04.920 --> 01:02:06.500
But like, you know, imagine if you could.

01:02:06.620 --> 01:02:07.460
Like that's really cool.

01:02:07.640 --> 01:02:11.560
And so I think that's kind of what we're getting here is like a glimpse of like Kojima thinking

01:02:11.560 --> 01:02:16.900
about video games in a way that not many people were thinking about at the time.

01:02:16.900 --> 01:02:20.640
And you can sort of see the little cogs of like how it all works and like, oh, this is

01:02:20.640 --> 01:02:22.620
what they were trying to accomplish by doing this little trick.

01:02:22.860 --> 01:02:31.040
Right. Because even the MSX version isn't flawless, you know, but there's a cohesion between story,

01:02:31.740 --> 01:02:38.900
gameplay, and level design here that is more advanced than say something like Super Mario

01:02:38.900 --> 01:02:40.900
Brothers, which nails everything it does.

01:02:40.900 --> 01:02:45.920
But maybe, you know, like after a while gets outdated of playing it a lot.

01:02:46.060 --> 01:02:50.080
Whereas in this one, you know, as long as you're not beating the game, you're constantly

01:02:50.080 --> 01:02:51.340
exploring like new stuff all the time.

01:02:51.340 --> 01:02:52.720
It feels fresh as you go.

01:02:53.140 --> 01:02:57.040
So I appreciate it, but sorry you're not making the essential games list.

